# Frontend JavaScript Parity From TypeScript

JavaScript components must mirror the canonical TypeScript component behavior and output.

## Source-of-Truth Rule

- Treat the TypeScript (`.ts`/`.tsx`) component as canonical.
- Do not introduce JS-only visual or behavioral divergence.
- Keep props, defaults, rendered structure, and interactions equivalent.

## Parity Requirements

- Maintain 1:1 Tailwind-to-MUI visual fidelity already established in TS.
- Match responsive behavior and all UI states (`hover`, `focus`, `active`, `disabled`).
- Preserve accessibility behavior and semantic markup.

## JavaScript Implementation Standards

- Use clear runtime defaults and prop destructuring to mirror TS intent.
- Add lightweight runtime safeguards where TS types previously enforced constraints.
- Keep naming and component exports aligned with TS counterparts for portability.

## Maintenance Rules

- When TS components change, update JS parity in the same work cycle.
- Document any unavoidable deltas and minimize them.
- Prefer consistency and predictability over JS-specific shortcuts.
